NAME
       df - show how much space mounted file systems have left

SYNOPSIS
       df [-h] [-H] [-T] [-i] [-t TYPE] [-x TYPE] [--output[=FIELDS]] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       df lists every mounted file system with its size, the space used and
       free, the percentage used, and where it is mounted:

           Filesystem            1K-blocks    Used Available Use% Mounted on
           /dev/mapper/rhel-root  17756160 2098764  15657396  12% /
           /dev/vda1               1038336  233268    805068  23% /boot

       With FILE arguments it shows only the file systems that hold those
       files, which tells you where a directory really lives.

       Sizes are in 1024-byte blocks unless -h or -H is given. The numbers are
       about the file system, not the device: use lsblk to see disks and
       partitions that are not mounted.

OPTIONS
       -h, --human-readable
              Sizes such as 2.0G and 512M, in powers of 1024.

       -H, --si
              The same in powers of 1000.

       -T, --print-type
              Add a Type column (xfs, ext4, vfat, nfs4, tmpfs...).

       -i, --inodes
              Show inodes (how many files can still be created) instead of
              blocks.

       -t TYPE, --type=TYPE
              Show only file systems of this type.

       -x TYPE, --exclude-type=TYPE
              Leave out file systems of this type (for example -x tmpfs).

       -l, --local
              Only local file systems (no NFS).

       -a, --all
              Include pseudo file systems such as proc and sysfs.

       -P, --portability
              One line per file system, in the POSIX format.

       --output[=FIELDS]
              Choose the columns, separated by commas: source, fstype,
              size, used, avail, pcent, target, itotal, iused, iavail,
              ipcent, file.

EXAMPLES
       Readable sizes and the file system types:

           df -hT

       Which file system holds /var/log, and how full is it?

           df -h /var/log

       Check that a new file system is mounted with the right size:

           df -h /data

       Only the mount points and free space:

           df --output=target,avail

SEE ALSO
       du(1), lsblk(8), findmnt(8), mount(8)
